    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The estimate your insurer sends is the foundation of your claim payment, yet most property owners receive it with little explanation. Line items carry abbreviations, quantities are in squares and linear feet, and the summary page subtracts depreciation and deductibles in ways that are hard to follow. An <strong>insurance estimate review in San Marcos</strong> compares that document with the damage at your property, item by item. As a San Marcos public adjuster, we read estimates the way insurers write them, so we can show you where the scope, quantities, or pricing may fall short.</p>

<h2>What We Look at in an Estimate Review</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Estimate section</th><th>Questions we ask</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Summary page</td><td>Are RCV, depreciation, deductible, and prior payments correct? Is depreciation marked recoverable where the policy allows?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Areas and structures</td><td>Is every damaged room, roof slope, elevation, and outbuilding included?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quantities</td><td>Do roof squares, waste factor, wall and ceiling area, and linear footage match measurements?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Line items</td><td>Are components such as starter, drip edge, flashing, vents, underlayment, trim, paint, and insulation present?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repair vs. replace</td><td>Does a spot repair make sense, or are materials discontinued, brittle, or unmatchable?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Is the price list region and date current? Do specialty items reflect local cost?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code and permits</td><td>Are code-required items included, and does ordinance or law coverage apply?</td></tr>
<tr><td>General conditions</td><td>Are debris removal, dumpsters, protection, permits, and contractor overhead and profit addressed?</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Common Gaps in San Marcos Estimates</h2>
<p>Every claim is different, but certain patterns come up often in Hays County properties:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Composition shingle roofs</strong> in subdivisions along I-35: missing starter strip, ridge cap, drip edge, step flashing, or a waste factor that is too low for a cut-up roof.</li>
<li><strong>Metal roofs</strong> on Hill Country homes, barns, and workshops west of town: hail dents treated as cosmetic, or panel replacement priced without trim and fasteners. Some policies limit cosmetic damage on metal roofs, so the policy wording matters.</li>
<li><strong>Stone, brick, and stucco exteriors:</strong> masonry and stucco repairs priced as if they were siding, or matching issues ignored.</li>
<li><strong>Older homes near downtown:</strong> plaster walls, wood windows, and trim profiles that standard line items do not capture.</li>
<li><strong>Water losses:</strong> drying equipment, baseboards, cabinets, and flooring continuity across rooms left out.</li>
<li><strong>Fences and other structures:</strong> wood and metal fences, sheds, and carports often omitted after hail or wind.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/estimate-software-roof-diagram.webp" alt="Adjuster using estimating software with a roof diagram on a laptop" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Roof diagrams and measurements are compared against the quantities in the insurer's estimate.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Understanding the Numbers on the Summary Page</h2>
<p>Many San Marcos homeowners call us because the check looks too small compared with the estimate total. Often the estimate math is working as designed: replacement cost minus depreciation gives actual cash value, then the deductible and any prior payments are subtracted. On many replacement cost policies, the withheld depreciation can be recovered once repairs are finished. Some Texas policies pay roofs only at actual cash value or on a payment schedule, which changes the picture. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> lets you check the arithmetic, and our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> explains depreciation in more detail.</p>
<p>A review separates two different problems: a correct estimate that simply pays in stages, and an estimate that leaves out real damage. The first may only need recoverable depreciation requested at the right time. The second needs documentation.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>How Our Estimate Review Works</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Gather the file.</strong> We collect the insurer's full estimate, photo report, payment letters, and your policy and declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect the property.</strong> We document damage with photos and measurements, using Matterport 3D imaging where it helps capture rooms or structures.</li>
<li><strong>Compare line by line.</strong> We note missing items, quantity differences, repair-vs-replace issues, and pricing concerns.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare our own estimate.</strong> We build a detailed Xactimate estimate so differences are visible in the same format the insurer uses.</li>
<li><strong>Explain the results.</strong> We walk you through what we found and what the options are, whether that is a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a>, a request on an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claim</a>, or, if the dispute is only about amount, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Sometimes the review shows the insurer's estimate is reasonable. That is useful to know too, and we will tell you plainly.</p>

<h2>Local Codes Can Change the Scope</h2>
<p>The City of San Marcos adopted the 2021 International Codes, in effect since April 1, 2023. A local amendment exempts roof covering replacement from permits unless the new roof is heavier than the existing one, and all foundations must be designed by a licensed engineer. Other repairs may require permits through the city <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1000/Permit-Center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Permit Center</a>. Code-driven costs are payable only to the extent your policy includes ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page has more.</p>

<h2>Get a Second Look at Your Estimate</h2>
<p>We serve property owners in San Marcos, Hays County, and surrounding Central Texas communities, for both <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential</a> and <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial</a> claims. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. We cannot guarantee an increase in your payment. To have a licensed <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> review your estimate, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
